  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The bullpen unraveled in the eighth inning and swallowed the lead whole.",
    "what_this_means": "Max Muncy kept them breathing with a two-run ninth, but late heroics don't erase the real failure: the game was already lost by then. Springs gave them five solid innings on a night when they needed six. The problem wasn't the starter. It was what came after. Juenger and Rom combined to give up seven runs in 1.0 inning, turning a 7-3 lead into a 9-7 deficit in one brutal stretch. Muncy's homer in the ninth made it close again, but close is not enough when you've already handed the game away. This team scored seven runs on eleven hits and somehow lost anyway. That's not inconsistency showing up late. That's collapse showing up when it hurts most. They built something real through seven innings and watched it evaporate because two relievers couldn't hold the moment. The margin between a win and a slide is narrow. And the bullpen just demonstrated exactly how fast it narrows.",
    "what_to_watch": "Randy Dobnak starts tonight with a 1.73 ERA, but the real test is whether Gage Jump can keep this one close enough to matter.",
